/// The dashboard theme CONTRACT — just the [[Theme]] class. Implementations
/// live in sibling modules, one per look:
///
///   - `theme-beer.pkl` — BeerCSS Material Design 3, the DEFAULT (wired in
///     `entry.pkl`) and currently the only shipped implementation.
///
/// A new theme is a sibling module exporting a `theme: Theme` instance; an
/// entry overrides its look by assigning `theme`, e.g.
/// `theme = (import "lib/theme-mine.pkl").theme`, and can retune any theme by
/// amending the instance (`tokens`/`styles` are ordinary properties; see the
/// implementation modules for their amend forms).
///
/// Everything visual lives in a theme, NOT in the server. The renderer emits
/// `tokens` as `:root` custom properties (with `tokensDark` under
/// prefers-color-scheme:dark); the server `<link>`s `stylesheets`, injects
/// `scripts` as ES modules, and inlines `styles` + `inlineScripts`.
///
/// Contract with the components/renderer (the class names they emit):
///   containers -> `.fh-grid` / `.fh-row` / `.fh-col`, the universal node
///   wrapper (the id'd flex/grid item the renderer puts around EVERY card) ->
///   `.fh-cell`, a query set's member container -> `.fh-group` — ALWAYS a
///   pure CSS passthrough (see [[layoutCss]]), not an author-facing choice,
///   per-cell layout classes -> `.fh-cols-<1..12>`/`.fh-cols-full` (authored
///   via the HA-style builders `columns(n)`/`fullWidth()` on
///   components.pkl [[LayoutNode]] — inert on a query set, which has no box
///   of its own to size), a grid's group-centering opt-out -> the
///   `.fh-start` marker on the grid element (`Grid.centered(false)`),
///   cards -> `.card`, section titles -> `.section`, state text -> `.state`,
///   popup dialog -> `.popup`/`.popup-close`, tabs -> `.tabs`/`.tabbar`/`.tab`,
///   more-info facts -> `.entity-info`/`.entity-info-id`/`.entity-info-attrs`
///   (the attribute block is preformatted text — a theme that does not wrap and
///   cap it gets a dialog as tall as the entity is chatty).
///   A guarded action while its POST is in flight toggles `.fh-busy` on the
///   tappable element itself (cards, buttons, pills, toggles, slider power
///   buttons — a theme should say "working", e.g. `cursor:progress` plus a
///   slight dim); a failed action shows `.fh-toast`, a small fixed bar created
///   by the shell on `datastar-fetch:error` — structure/behavior live in the
///   shell, the LOOK here.
///
/// The `fh-` LAYOUT classes have ready-made framework-agnostic CSS below
/// ([[layoutCss]]): a theme normally interpolates it at the top of its
/// `styles` (`\(themeMod.layoutCss)`) and only re-implements it to change the
/// layout system itself. The visual classes (`.card`, `.section`, …) are the
/// part each theme styles its own way.
///
/// WRITING A THEME (the whole API surface):
///   1. Create a sibling `theme-<name>.pkl` exporting `theme: Theme` (or an
///      `open class` extending [[Theme]] if you want amendable knobs, like
///      theme-beer's palettes).
///   2. `tokens`/`tokensDark`: HA-named design tokens (see tokens.pkl) —
///      emitted as `:root` custom properties, dark under
///      prefers-color-scheme.
///   3. `stylesheets`/`scripts`: your framework's CDN links (pinned versions,
///      never `@latest`). Scripts are ES modules injected after stylesheets;
///      dashboard BEHAVIOR stays with Datastar — ship JS only for visuals
///      your CSS needs (e.g. BeerCSS's slider track fill), and verify its
///      bindings survive SSE morphs (delegated/document-level listeners do).
///      `inlineScripts` is the same rule for JS you write yourself rather than
///      link — a pointer gesture your rules depend on, like
///      [[sliderHoldScript]]. Both force a page RELOAD when they change (a
///      script cannot be un-run), unlike `styles`, which patches.
///   4. `styles`: start with `\(themeMod.layoutCss)` (the layout contract),
///      then style the visual classes from the roster above on top of the
///      tokens.
///   5. `chrome`: the page frame — MUST wrap `{{{body}}}` in an element with
///      `id="dashboard"` (the reload swap target; enforced by
///      Dashboard.validate) and, if the dashboard uses popups, host a
///      `<div id="popups">{{{popups}}}</div>` overlay mount. The hole is where
///      a popup being RESTORED on a refresh is baked, so it is on screen with
///      the first paint instead of arriving when the stream connects; leave it
///      out and the dialog still works, it just pops in a moment late.
///   6. An entry opts in with `theme = (import "lib/theme-<name>.pkl").theme`.
module theme

/// Framework-agnostic CSS for the `fh-` layout contract — the sane default
/// every theme can reuse. Three container behaviors, mirroring HA's
/// "sections" dashboard (see ADR 0008):
///
///   - GRID (`.fh-grid`, the default top-level container): a 12-column
///     sizing system where a cell's width is its `columns(n)` span (default
///     4 — a third, HA's default card size) of 12. Built on flexbox rather
///     than CSS grid so a PARTIAL row (cells not summing to 12) group-centers
///     while a FULL row fills edge-to-edge — the HA-sections feel. Centering
///     is ON by default; `Grid.centered(false)` emits `.fh-start` to
///     left-pack instead. The span basis `(100% + gap) * n/12 - gap` makes
///     any set of spans summing to 12 fit one row exactly (the `+gap`/`-gap`
///     cancels the inter-cell gaps).
///   - ROW (`.fh-row`): a horizontal flex line whose children SHARE the width
///     equally (`flex:1 1 0`). No 12-col spans here — sizing is share-based.
///   - COLUMN (`.fh-col`): a vertical flex stack whose children FILL the width.
///
/// A cell normally FILLS its share — the width its container hands it — which is
/// what every card wants. `.fh-hug` (`LayoutNode.hug()`) is the opt-out: the cell
/// shrinks to its content, so a run of them packs at the container's own gap
/// instead of each one rattling around inside an equal share. It is what makes a
/// row of pills read as a row of pills; a filling card in a grid still needs a
/// SPAN, so `fh-hug` and `fh-cols-*` are different questions and compose.
///
/// **A container states its children's layout as an INHERITED CUSTOM PROPERTY
/// (`--fh-cell-flex`/`--fh-cell-align-self`/`--fh-cell-width`), not a
/// child-combinator selector** — every `.fh-cell` just reads
/// `flex:var(--fh-cell-flex,…)` from whichever ancestor last declared it. A
/// container only ever says "this is my children's layout"; it never reaches
/// for a child directly, which is what makes a query set's `.fh-group`
/// member container composable rather than a fixed flow of its own: it is
/// ALWAYS `display:contents` (a pure CSS passthrough — it must stay in the
/// DOM, the runtime's per-member patch target, ADR 0003, but need not
/// generate a box), so its members always resolve their flex/width from
/// whichever REAL container — `Row`, `Column`, `Grid` — actually holds them,
/// at ANY nesting depth, because custom-property inheritance crosses a
/// `display:contents` element exactly like any other. This is internal to
/// how a set renders, not an authoring choice: there is nothing to opt into,
/// and no flow a set could sensibly keep of its own once it always defers to
/// its real container. A `.fh-row>.fh-cell` CHILD-COMBINATOR selector could
/// not do this — selectors match the actual DOM tree, and `.fh-group` stays
/// in it (nothing else could then target it by id), so the combinator would
/// stop at the wrapper instead of reaching its grandchildren.
///
/// So `columns(n)`/`fullWidth()` are GRID-ONLY affordances; inside a row/column
/// you don't size children, they share/fill (and on a query set, calling them
/// is simply inert — a set has no box of its own to size). Rows and columns
/// nest freely into each other and into grid cells. On narrow screens
/// (<=640px) every grid cell collapses to full width, a set's members
/// included (`.fh-group>.fh-cell` is a DOM selector, unaffected by
/// `display:contents`, so it still reaches them). The default-span selectors
/// use `:where()` so any authored `.fh-cols-<n>` beats them (as does
/// `--fh-cell-flex` itself: real class selectors, `.fh-cols-<n>` included,
/// always outrank `:where()`'s zero specificity); `--fh-gap` retunes spacing
/// per theme.
const layoutCss: String = """
  .fh-grid{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;justify-content:center;gap:var(--fh-gap,1rem);--fh-cell-flex:0 1 calc((100% + var(--fh-gap,1rem)) * 4 / 12 - var(--fh-gap,1rem))}
  .fh-grid.fh-start{justify-content:flex-start}
  .fh-group{display:contents}
  :where(.fh-cell){flex:var(--fh-cell-flex,0 1 auto);min-width:0;align-self:var(--fh-cell-align-self,stretch);width:var(--fh-cell-width,auto)}
  """ + colSpans + """

  .fh-cols-full{flex-basis:100%}
  .fh-row{display:flex;gap:var(--fh-gap,1rem);flex-wrap:wrap;--fh-cell-flex:1 1 0;--fh-cell-align-self:flex-start}
  .fh-cell.fh-hug{flex:0 0 auto}
  .fh-col{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--fh-gap,1rem);--fh-cell-width:100%}
  .fh-cell:has(>.fh-ifhost:empty){display:none}
  @media (max-width:640px){.fh-grid>.fh-cell,.fh-group>.fh-cell{flex-basis:100%}}
  """

// One span rule per grid column count (`.fh-cols-1` .. `.fh-cols-12`): the
// flex-basis that makes an n/12 cell fit (gaps cancel — see [[layoutCss]]).
const local colSpans: String = new Listing {
  for (n in IntSeq(1, 12)) {
    ".fh-cols-\(n){flex-basis:calc((100% + var(--fh-gap,1rem)) * \(n) / 12 - var(--fh-gap,1rem))}"
  }
}.toList().join("\n")

/// Press-and-hold before a TOUCH may move a slider, so a dashboard of slider
/// cards is still a scrollable list. Without it every swipe that happens to
/// start on a card sets a brightness, and a tap meant to stop a fling sets one
/// too. Pair it with the CSS half — on coarse pointers the range input must be
/// `pointer-events:none` and its wrapper `touch-action:pan-y` (see
/// theme-beer.pkl); `.fh-armed` lands on the wrapper while the hold is live,
/// for whatever affordance the theme wants.
///
/// The value is driven from here rather than handed to the range input after
/// the hold, because it cannot be handed over: a synthesized `pointerdown` is
/// untrusted, and untrusted events do not start a native drag. So this maps
/// x -> value itself, dispatching `input` (which repaints the fill and updates
/// the bound signal) and, on release, `change` (the card's `data-on:change`
/// POST). Mouse gestures are left entirely alone.
///
/// `touch-action` is latched when a gesture begins, so the wrapper stays
/// `pan-y` even while armed: a vertical move still scrolls and cancels the
/// pointer, which disarms. That is the intended escape, not a gap.
///
/// Keyed on `.slider.max`, which the slider CARD emits (components.pkl), so it
/// is reusable by any theme that keeps that markup — the same deal as
/// [[layoutCss]].
const sliderHoldScript: String = """
  (()=>{const H=350,S=8;
  let t=null,w=null,i=null,x0=0,y0=0,pid=0;
  const off=()=>{if(t){clearTimeout(t);t=null}
  if(w){w.classList.remove('fh-armed');try{w.releasePointerCapture(pid)}catch(e){}}
  w=null;i=null};
  const put=(cx,commit)=>{if(!w||!i)return;const r=w.getBoundingClientRect();if(!r.width)return;
  const mn=+i.min||0,mx=+i.max||100,st=+i.step||1,
  p=Math.min(1,Math.max(0,(cx-r.left)/r.width)),
  v=Math.min(mx,Math.max(mn,Math.round((mn+p*(mx-mn))/st)*st));
  if(v!=+i.value){i.value=v;i.dispatchEvent(new Event('input',{bubbles:true}))}
  if(commit)i.dispatchEvent(new Event('change',{bubbles:true}))};
  document.addEventListener('pointerdown',e=>{if(e.pointerType==='mouse')return;
  const el=e.target&&e.target.closest&&e.target.closest('.slider.max');if(!el)return;
  off();const inp=el.querySelector('input[type=range]');if(!inp)return;
  w=el;i=inp;x0=e.clientX;y0=e.clientY;pid=e.pointerId;
  t=setTimeout(()=>{t=null;if(!w)return;w.classList.add('fh-armed');
  try{w.setPointerCapture(pid)}catch(e){}
  if(navigator.vibrate)navigator.vibrate(8);put(x0,false)},H)},true);
  document.addEventListener('pointermove',e=>{if(!w)return;
  if(t){if(Math.abs(e.clientX-x0)>S||Math.abs(e.clientY-y0)>S)off();return}
  put(e.clientX,false)},true);
  const end=e=>{if(!w)return;if(!t&&e.type==='pointerup')put(e.clientX,true);off()};
  document.addEventListener('pointerup',end,true);
  document.addEventListener('pointercancel',end,true)})();
  """

open class Theme {
  tokens: Mapping<String, String>
  tokensDark: Mapping<String, String>
  stylesheets: Listing<String>
  /// External JS URLs, `<script type="module" src>`-injected in the document
  /// head (after the stylesheets, before Datastar). ES modules — deferred, so
  /// they run after the first paint. For framework helpers a theme's CSS needs
  /// (e.g. BeerCSS's slider fill); dashboard behavior stays Datastar's.
  scripts: Listing<String> = new {}
  /// Classic `<script>` bodies inlined in the head, run BEFORE first paint —
  /// the gesture half of a CSS interaction, for what CSS alone cannot express
  /// (see [[sliderHoldScript]]). Dashboard BEHAVIOR still stays Datastar's:
  /// this is for a pointer gesture the theme's own rules depend on, not for
  /// talking to the server.
  inlineScripts: Listing<String> = new {}
  styles: String
  chrome: String
}
